# <name> — environment configuration
#
# Copy this file to .env and fill in real values:
#   cp .env.example .env
#
# .env is gitignored. Never commit real secrets.
#
# ── How this file is organised ─────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# The block at the BOTTOM, between the `scaffold:env` markers, is GENERATED
# from the config module (<config_module>) — the single place every variable
# this project reads is declared. Do not edit it by hand: add a field to the
# config module and run `make env-example`.
#
# Everything ABOVE that block is hand-maintained: operator notes and optional
# add-on wiring for infrastructure the core project does not require.
#
#   make env-example   regenerate the managed block
#   make check-env     fail if the block and the config module disagree

# ── Jupyter authentication (notebook shape only) ──────────────────────────
# Localhost-first use is tokenless by default. Set a strong token before
# exposing Jupyter through any reverse proxy; an unauthenticated Jupyter
# server grants visitors arbitrary code execution inside the container.
# JUPYTER_TOKEN=

# ── External APIs (replace placeholders with real keys) ────────────────────
# Prefer the Vault add-on (`--with-vault`) over pasting long-lived keys here:
# `make secrets-pull` fetches them into .env at mode 0600, and rotation
# becomes one command instead of an archaeology exercise.
# OPENAI_API_KEY=replace_me
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=replace_me

# ── Feature flags ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# ENABLE_DEBUG=false

# ── Vault (only with --with-vault; see vault/README.md) ───────────────────
# Discovery is by environment variable, never a hardcoded path, so a
# generated project stays portable and publishable.
#
# VAULT_ADDR       Vault API address, e.g. http://127.0.0.1:8200
# VAULT_ROLE       AppRole this project authenticates as
# VAULT_KV_MOUNT   KV v2 mount name (default: kv)
# VAULT_CREDS_DIR  Where role-id/secret-id live (default: ~/.vault)
#
# VAULT_ADDR=http://127.0.0.1:8200
# VAULT_ROLE=<name>
# VAULT_KV_MOUNT=kv

# ── RAG (only used when scaffolded with --with-rag) ───────────────────────
# Backend: sqlite (no infrastructure) | remote (operator's vector DB) | both
RAG_BACKEND=sqlite
# VECTOR_DB_URL=http://127.0.0.1:6333
# VECTOR_DB_EMBEDDING_MODEL=bge-m3

# ── Per-project Gitea (only with --with-gitea; AGENTS.md §24) ─────────────
# Each scaffolded project has its own Gitea container where dispatched
# sub-agents push their branches and the review agent merges/rejects PRs.
# `make gitea` starts the container; `make gitea-bootstrap` creates the
# bot user, API token, and seeds the repo on first run.
#
# HTTP + SSH ports (change per project if running multiple on the same host).
# Note: 2222 commonly collides with operator tools (GitLab, vagrant), so the
# default is 2230. Only used if you need SSH git access — the bot workflow
# uses HTTPS + API token and doesn't need this port at all.
GITEA_HTTP_PORT=3030
GITEA_SSH_PORT=2230
#
# Public URL exposed via your central Caddy reverse proxy. When set, the
# Gitea container's ROOT_URL uses this, and the bot posts clickable PR
# URLs in IRC. See gitea/caddy.example for the Caddyfile snippet the
# operator pastes into their central Caddy config.
#
# Convention: `<slug>-git.<your-public-domain>` where <slug> matches the
# project name AND the IRC channel. Operators who expose openclaw-managed
# projects under a dedicated .dev zone should set GITEA_PUBLIC_DOMAIN and
# the scaffold composes GITEA_PUBLIC_URL automatically.
#
# GITEA_PUBLIC_DOMAIN=your-dev-zone.example
# GITEA_PUBLIC_URL=https://<name>-git.your-dev-zone.example
#
# These are populated automatically by gitea/bootstrap.sh on first run —
# don't set them by hand unless you're pointing at a shared upstream Gitea.
# GITEA_URL=http://127.0.0.1:3030
# GITEA_API_URL=http://127.0.0.1:3030/api/v1
# GITEA_TOKEN=
# GITEA_OWNER=<name>-bot
# GITEA_REPO=<name>
# GITEA_BOT_USER=<name>-bot
# GITEA_BOT_PASSWORD=
#
# Optional Gitea security keys (if empty, Gitea generates them on first run)
# GITEA_SECRET_KEY=
# GITEA_INTERNAL_TOKEN=
#
# Review agent behavior — the bot's review_pr tool reads these
# GITEA_AUTO_REVIEW=false              # automatically review PRs after dispatch
# GITEA_AUTO_MERGE_CONFIDENCE=high     # min confidence for auto-merge verdict (high|medium|low)
# REVIEW_MAX_DIFF_CHARS=40000          # cap diff size sent to the LLM reviewer

# ── Pipeline self-awareness + auto-fix loop (AGENTS.md §24.5) ──────────────
# The orchestration bot watches Gitea Actions pipelines after opening a PR.
# If the pipeline fails, it captures the failure as a memory and dispatches
# a new sub-agent to fix it (up to AUTOFIX_MAX_ATTEMPTS times, tracked in
# the root sub_agent_run memory). Review agent blocks merges on red pipelines.
# PIPELINE_WAIT_TIMEOUT=600            # max seconds to wait for a pipeline to finish
# PIPELINE_POLL_INTERVAL=15            # seconds between pipeline status polls
# AUTOFIX_MAX_ATTEMPTS=3               # circuit breaker: max auto-fix dispatches per root task
#
# Gitea Actions runner token — populated by gitea/bootstrap.sh on first run.
# GITEA_RUNNER_TOKEN=
# GITEA_RUNNER_NAME=<name>-runner

# ── Memory: two-tier (project-local + long-term cross-project) ─────────────
# Project memories live in memory/ (committed). The long-term store is
# optional — if set, the bot can query cross-project lessons via the
# read_memory_longterm tool, and `make memory-promote` pushes vetted project
# memories to the shared store. See AGENTS.md §23 for the full flow.
#
# MEMORY_STORE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:7140
# MEMORY_STORE_API_KEY=
# Auto-promote high-confidence memories without waiting for operator review
# (leave false in production — review gate catches bad promotions).
# MEMORY_AUTO_PROMOTE=false
# Minimum confidence required for auto-promote (high | medium | low)
# MEMORY_MIN_CONFIDENCE=high

# ── Docker networks the containers join ────────────────────────────────────
# These are DOCKER NETWORK NAMES, not hostnames. The compose files refer to
# them by a fixed alias (irc_default, rag_network) and map the alias onto the
# real network via `name:`. If a stack fails to start with
#   "network X declared as external, but could not be found"
# the value here does not match a real network. List them with:
#   docker network ls
# IRC_NETWORK=irc-server_default
# RAG_NETWORK=production-agentic-rag-course_rag-network

# ── IRC bot (only with --with-bot; see AGENTS.md §22) ─────────────────────
# All of these are operator-supplied. They are ignored unless the bot add-on
# was explicitly included.
#
# IRC_SERVER=irc.example.com
# IRC_PORT=6667
# IRC_TLS=false
# IRC_NICK=<name>-bot
# IRC_CHANNEL=#<name>
# IRC_PASSWORD=
# AGENTS_CHANNEL=#agents
# ADVISOR_CHANNEL=#devs
# ADVISOR_NICK=design-advisor

# LLM endpoint for the bot's tool-calling loop (OpenAI-compatible):
# works with Ollama, llm-router, llama.cpp, vLLM, LiteLLM, real OpenAI, etc.
# LLM_ROUTER_URL=https://openrouter.ai/api/v1
# LLM_MODEL=nvidia/nemotron-3-super-120b-a12b:free
# LLM_API_KEY=


# ── Bot agent dispatch (only with --with-bot) ──────────────────────────────
# The bot can dispatch coding agents to complete tasks on throwaway git
# branches. Sub-agents ALWAYS run inside a hard-isolated Docker sandbox
# (AGENTS.md §22): only the project root is mounted, no network access by
# default, read-only rootfs, cpu/memory capped, no host credentials. If the
# sub-agent needs something beyond the project folder, the ORCHESTRATION
# bot composes the needed capability from its own tools — the sub-agent
# never reaches out directly.
#
# Configure by picking a runner:
#     AGENT_DISPATCHER=cursor      && AGENT_COMMAND=./bot/runners/cursor.sh
#     AGENT_DISPATCHER=claude-code && AGENT_COMMAND=./bot/runners/claude_code.sh
#     AGENT_DISPATCHER=aider       && AGENT_COMMAND=./bot/runners/aider.sh
#     AGENT_DISPATCHER=custom      && AGENT_COMMAND=./bot/runners/custom.sh
#
# Leave empty to disable agent dispatch entirely.
# AGENT_DISPATCHER=
# AGENT_COMMAND=
# AGENT_MAX_CONCURRENT=3
# AGENT_TIMEOUT_S=1800
# AGENT_BRANCH_PREFIX=bot/

# ── Sandbox isolation for dispatched sub-agents (AGENTS.md §22) ────────────
# AGENT_SANDBOX=docker (default) wraps every sub-agent dispatch in a
# locked-down container. AGENT_SANDBOX=none disables the sandbox — ONLY
# use this for local development. Production ALWAYS uses docker.
# AGENT_SANDBOX=docker
#
# Required when AGENT_SANDBOX=docker — the image containing your coding
# agent CLI. Operator builds this once per agent flavor:
#     docker build -t ghcr.io/you/agent-sandbox-cursor:latest \
#                  -f bot/sandbox/cursor.Dockerfile .
# AGENT_SANDBOX_IMAGE=ghcr.io/you/agent-sandbox:latest
#
# Network isolation level: none (default, no outbound) | host (dev) |
# bridge | a custom docker network. 'none' is the strict default — if the
# sub-agent needs LLM access, either bake a local model into the sandbox
# image or use a restricted bridge with an egress proxy pointing only at
# the LLM endpoint.
# AGENT_SANDBOX_NETWORK=none
#
# Resource caps
# AGENT_SANDBOX_CPUS=2
# AGENT_SANDBOX_MEMORY=2g
#
# Env var whitelist (only these are forwarded into the sandbox — everything
# else is stripped so operator secrets don't leak in)
# AGENT_SANDBOX_FORWARD_ENV=OPENAI_API_KEY,ANTHROPIC_API_KEY,OPENAI_BASE_URL,LLM_ROUTER_URL,LLM_MODEL

# ── AI code review (only with --with-review; see review/README.md) ────────
# An OpenAI- or Anthropic-compatible endpoint. Any provider speaking that
# protocol works, including a local vLLM/Ollama server — which keeps your
# code on your own hardware.
#
# OCR_LLM_URL=https://api.openai.com/v1/chat/completions
# OCR_LLM_AUTH_TOKEN=replace_me
# OCR_LLM_MODEL=gpt-4o
# OCR_USE_ANTHROPIC=false
#
# Lowest severity that fails the job: critical | high | medium | low | none.
# Defaults to critical, so red always means something — see review/README.md.
# OCR_FAIL_ON=critical
